WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.700
Rotation limits damage from exposed, stale, overused, or broadly shared keys.

2
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:08.440
But rotation is not just a policy statement.

3
00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:12.460
It is an engineering capability that has to work before an emergency.

4
00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:24.080
A team that has never rotated a JWT signing key, HMAC webhook secret, database encryption key, or service credential may discover during an incident that rotation breaks production.

5
00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:29.240
Some keys can be rotated by issuing a new key and invalidating the old one.

6
00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.060
Others require staged rollout.

7
00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:36.960
Verifiers may need to accept both old and new signing keys during a transition.

8
00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:44.260
Encrypted records may need key identifiers or version metadata so the application can choose the right decryption key.

9
00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:48.920
Old keys may need a controlled decrypt-only period before retirement.

10
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.640
Envelope encryption can make rotation more practical.

11
00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:56.260
Data encryption keys protect data.

12
00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.320
Key encryption keys protect those data keys.

13
00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:05.820
Rotating a key encryption key may involve rewrapping data keys instead of re-encrypting every record.

14
00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:13.040
This can reduce operational pain while still supporting separation by record, tenant, service, or purpose.

15
00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:18.740
The pattern still needs metadata, ownership, audit, and recovery rules.

16
00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:24.620
Rotation should include validation that old keys no longer grant unintended access.

17
00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:36.000
That may mean checking expired JWT signing keys, retired webhook secrets, invalidated API keys, old object-storage credentials, or data keys after rewrap.

18
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:39.620
Revocation should be explicit when compromise is suspected.

19
00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:46.920
The question is not whether rotation is written in a policy; the question is whether the team can execute it safely under pressure.
